===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|aʊ|lang=de}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{term|göu|lang=gmh}}, {{term|gou|lang=gmh}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|gawi|lang=goh}}, {{term|gouwi|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*gawi}} (compare Low German {{term|Gohe|lang=nds}}, Dutch {{term|gouw|lang=nl}}, West Frisian {{term|gea|lang=fy}}, {{term|goa|lang=fy}}), from earlier {{term/t|de|*gaawja}} ‘riverlands, lakeside land’, from collective {{term/t|gem-pro|*ga-}} + {{term/t|gem-pro|*awjō}} ‘land by a waterway’ (compare German {{term|Aue|lang=de}}).

====Proper noun====
{{de-noun|m|Gaus|gen2=Gaues|Gaue}}

# A [[shire]] or [[district]]; especially a district in the German [[Reich]], 1939-1945.

====Derived terms====
* [[Gauleiter]]

===Etymology 2===
From the acronym [[GAU]], now used colloquially and written as a single word.

====Proper noun====
{{de-noun|m|Gaus|gen2=Gaues|Gaue}}

# An accident, a calamity

:''Außerdem neigen die Blutplättchen von depressiven Menschen eher dazu, sich zu Gerinnseln zusammenzuballen. Diese können irgendwann wichtige Adern verstopfen und einen Gau im Herzen oder Hirn auslösen.'' [http://www.persoenlichkeits-blog.de/article/7114/psychokardiologiewie-herzinfarkt-und-depression-zusammenhang]

